1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,559
Lina: "I have merely been transplanted from one scene of student-life to another." That's a young man writing from Cambridge in 1859.

2
00:00:08,559 --> 00:00:14,406
Maya: Transplanted. That's such a brave word for, "I already miss home."

3
00:00:14,406 --> 00:00:22,886
Theo: And we have that sentence because the letter became a book, the book became a scan, and the scan became crawler food.

4
00:00:22,886 --> 00:00:24,806
Maya: Crawler food?

5
00:00:24,806 --> 00:00:30,806
Theo: That's the technical term. Pen to pixel in one hundred and fifty-four years.

6
00:00:30,806 --> 00:00:41,365
Lina: And after all that survival, what survives emotionally is so simple: he knows home is about to become somewhere he visits.

7
00:00:41,365 --> 00:00:48,649
Maya: the idea of going away for good and all, and always hereafter being at home only as a visitor.

8
00:00:48,649 --> 00:00:52,332
Theo: That phrase gets worse the longer you sit with it.

9
00:00:52,332 --> 00:00:56,812
Maya: Welcome to The Homes We Leave Behind. I'm Maya.

10
00:00:56,812 --> 00:00:57,452
Theo: I'm Theo.

11
00:00:57,452 --> 00:00:58,651
Lina: And I'm Lina.

12
00:00:58,651 --> 00:01:06,331
Theo: Quick note on where all this comes from: every source today was grabbed by a web crawler in December 2013.

13
00:01:06,331 --> 00:01:12,012
Lina: That's when it was captured, not necessarily when it was written. That 1859 letter?

14
00:01:12,012 --> 00:01:22,092
Maya: It was sitting on the Internet Archive as a raw text file—an OCR dump of a scanned book, complete with broken line breaks and a stray page number header.

15
00:01:22,092 --> 00:01:26,172
Lina: So December is the shelf date, not the birth date.

16
00:01:26,172 --> 00:01:32,253
Maya: Today we're asking what happens when the place you call home becomes a place you can only visit.

17
00:01:32,253 --> 00:01:40,332
Theo: And that ache turned up everywhere in this archive—old letters, blog posts, a grief journal on a health website.

18
00:01:40,332 --> 00:01:50,012
Lina: A century and a half later, a blogger chooses the name Saudade—a word for longing that never quite resolves.

19
00:01:50,012 --> 00:01:53,213
Maya: Wait. They named the blog after the feeling?

20
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:59,920
Lina: Exactly. They had left Cape Verde, and years later they still thought about it every day.

21
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:07,519
Theo: So one writer calls leaving a transplant. Another names the whole archive after the ache.

22
00:02:07,519 --> 00:02:16,640
Theo: There never was a time when I was more myself, more comfortable with the skin I wore, the people I'd seen, the place I'd visited.

23
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:24,160
Lina: What gets me is that Saudade's home isn't the place they grew up. It's a place they chose and then had to leave.

24
00:02:24,160 --> 00:02:28,479
Maya: And that kind of homesickness—try explaining that to someone.

25
00:02:28,479 --> 00:02:34,320
Theo: The blog itself is a little mystery. The archive shows exactly one post.

26
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:46,079
Lina: It's called "Raskalnikov"—yes, Dostoevsky—but the display name is Saudade. Two literary personas are sharing one page.

27
00:02:46,079 --> 00:02:50,079
Maya: And the Cape Verdean Creole isn't translated for outsiders?

28
00:02:50,079 --> 00:02:54,800
Theo: No. That part of the page stays with the people who already understand it.

29
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:57,360
Maya: Maybe they weren't writing for us.

30
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:04,081
Theo: Maybe they were writing for the friends they name at the end of the post—people still in Cape Verde who'd understand every word.

31
00:03:04,081 --> 00:03:08,480
Lina: Okay, now here's where Theo found something that floored me.

32
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:10,800
Maya: In another OCR scan?

33
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:16,881
Lina: Another scan, page numbers and all. And this one is a letter from Beethoven.

34
00:03:16,881 --> 00:03:19,360
Maya: Wait. Beethoven?

35
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:30,321
Theo: It's addressed to Stephan von Breuning. Beethoven is writing about how his deafness keeps him from traveling—he can't go see the scenery of his fatherland, he can't marry.

36
00:03:30,321 --> 00:03:32,801
Lina: And then there's this line—

37
00:03:32,801 --> 00:03:41,760
Lina: Oh ! I could span the world were I only free from this ! I feel that my youth is only now commencing.

38
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:50,001
Maya: He's homesick for a life he can't get to. Not a specific house—a whole future his deafness has walled off.

39
00:03:50,880 --> 00:04:02,960
Theo: And the digital version of the letter just cuts off mid-sentence. His closing words, his signature—gone. The scan or the extraction stopped before the end.

40
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:08,881
Lina: So even this archived copy of a published copy of a private letter is incomplete.

41
00:04:08,881 --> 00:04:15,921
Lina: The silence belongs to the file, not necessarily to him.

42
00:04:15,921 --> 00:04:25,760
Lina: Then there's Brooke Davidoff. And her story brings all of this into the present tense. She wrote a post called "Homesick" after her father died.

43
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:30,801
Maya: She's living far from southern California, where her mother and her father's house are.

44
00:04:30,801 --> 00:04:38,001
Theo: The family owns the house in Wildomar together, but nobody can afford the mortgage, and nobody lives nearby.

45
00:04:38,001 --> 00:04:45,921
Lina: I want to go home. The California heat and my mommy are calling. I'm not homesick, I'm heart sick.

46
00:04:45,921 --> 00:04:54,081
Theo: Her post cuts off mid-sentence too—she starts describing a dream at a party and the text just stops.

47
00:04:54,081 --> 00:04:58,641
Lina: We don't know if that's how she left it or if the crawl missed the rest.

48
00:04:58,641 --> 00:05:04,720
Lina: Twice now, the archive has closed the door before the ending.

49
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:15,361
Maya: Four voices across two centuries. And every one of them is reaching toward a home that's already changed—or that they've changed too much to simply walk back into.

50
00:05:15,361 --> 00:05:23,360
Lina: But distance does something stranger, too—it enlarges the ordinary. That's where we're headed next.

51
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:31,680
Maya: So we've been talking about what it means to leave. But distance does this strange thing — it doesn't just separate you from a place.

52
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:36,000
Theo: It takes every ordinary thing you left behind and makes it radiant.

53
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:43,920
Lina: And what he's longing for isn't grand at all. It's voices. Songs. Faces.

54
00:05:43,920 --> 00:05:52,479
Maya: I sigh for rest and the sound of home
voices, the music of home songs, the light of loved eyes and
faces

55
00:05:52,479 --> 00:06:00,320
Theo: He pictures himself on a little island in the boundless ocean, surrounded by travelers gambling over champagne on a Sunday.

56
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:03,280
Maya: The Sunday champagne really bothered him.

57
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:11,919
Lina: He is also harsh about foreign faces and tongues in ways that are very much of his era. That's his period-bound lens, not ours.

58
00:06:11,919 --> 00:06:22,960
Maya: But that impulse — cataloguing exactly what you miss — it doesn't go away. A blogger named Emily does the same thing in 2012, from Kenya.

59
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:32,079
Theo: She's on a site called One Trailing Spouse, and she writes this list of five things she misses about the U.S. after five months abroad.

60
00:06:32,079 --> 00:06:35,839
Lina: And the list is so specific it's almost funny.

61
00:06:35,839 --> 00:06:38,319
Maya: How specific are we talking?

62
00:06:38,319 --> 00:06:43,627
Theo: Purple corduroy pants. Her muffin tin. Netflix.

63
00:06:43,627 --> 00:06:46,508
Maya: The muffin tin is the one that gets me.

64
00:06:46,508 --> 00:06:56,027
Lina: And driving — just the freedom to get in a car by herself after dark. She's candid that this is a privileged perspective, she says so.

65
00:06:56,027 --> 00:07:01,228
Maya: But missing the feeling of casual safety? That's real no matter how you frame it.

66
00:07:01,228 --> 00:07:12,534
Maya: A commenter named Marina chimes in from England and says she hasn't worn completely dry clothes in nearly two years. She misses the sun. And avocados.

67
00:07:12,534 --> 00:07:17,014
Theo: Honestly? Avocados can do that.

68
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:29,040
Lina: Then there's Kathleen, on a WordPress blog called My 8×10 Life. She grew up in the rural Midwest, moved reluctantly to Brooklyn.

69
00:07:29,040 --> 00:07:39,440
Maya: And she writes about driving back through those Midwestern landscapes and feeling like she could finally stretch and breathe again — after months of swallowing the grit of New York.

70
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:47,200
Maya: But she says something that really gets me. She talks about the details of home growing fuzzy, and it bothers her.

71
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:53,279
Theo: Like the feelings of belonging are still sharp, but the specifics — they're slipping away.

72
00:07:53,279 --> 00:08:04,400
Lina: But, I don’t think I’ll ever allow myself to like it, and will never love it no matter how well I adapt to the urban life. I guess it makes me feel disloyal to my roots.

73
00:08:04,400 --> 00:08:14,800
Theo: Disloyal. That word. Homesickness isn't just missing a place — it becomes a kind of moral obligation to keep missing it.

74
00:08:14,800 --> 00:08:27,600
Maya: We found another exile — a memoir fragment from the Internet Archive's OCR pipeline. Someone marking a thirty-seventh birthday in May 1919, living in seclusion on an island.

75
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:38,161
Theo: The narrator doesn't fully identify themselves in this fragment, but letters and flowers arrive from Germany — so many the small rooms of a parsonage can't hold them all.

76
00:08:38,161 --> 00:08:45,601
Lina: What struck me is how the narrator measures belonging — not by being present, but by being remembered.

77
00:08:45,601 --> 00:08:55,201
Maya: Those letters prove people still feel connected across distance and, as the writer puts it, across a wildly raging campaign of slander.

78
00:08:55,201 --> 00:09:05,281
Theo: And we should be honest — we don't know from this fragment alone who the narrator is or what the slander was about. The text starts mid-sentence.

79
00:09:05,281 --> 00:09:08,081
Lina: That's the nature of digitized scraps.

80
00:09:08,081 --> 00:09:10,880
Lina: A gap is not permission to invent.

81
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:15,761
Maya: Which brings us to a passage that kind of names the whole feeling we've been circling.

82
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:25,360
Theo: From what appears to be volume twenty-one of the collected works of someone abbreviated 'Stev' in the filename — an essay about roads.

83
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:31,119
Lina: And the writer introduces this German word: Sehnsucht.

84
00:09:31,119 --> 00:09:36,000
Lina: The passion for what is ever beyond. That ache for the horizon.

85
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:41,360
Theo: And the essay quotes Goethe's Werther to make a devastating point.

86
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:50,752
Lina: When you finally reach the distance you wanted—when 'there' becomes 'here'—everything is afterwards as it was before.

87
00:09:50,752 --> 00:09:54,271
Maya: So arrival can make the dream smaller?

88
00:09:54,271 --> 00:09:58,911
Theo: Exactly. You arrive, and the magic collapses.

89
00:09:58,911 --> 00:10:05,312
Maya: Wheeler sighing for home voices. Kathleen gripping the steering wheel through flat fields.

90
00:10:05,312 --> 00:10:11,792
Lina: Emily dreaming of purple pants. The unnamed exile counting birthday flowers.

91
00:10:11,792 --> 00:10:17,473
Theo: They're all living inside that word: Sehnsucht.

92
00:10:18,320 --> 00:10:24,959
Maya: But Sehnsucht changes when the distant place becomes a destination. What happens when you actually go back?

93
00:10:24,959 --> 00:10:28,400
Theo: So we found a story in the crawl that lands right on this.

94
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:41,520
Lina: First-person essay by Christal Presley—originally published on CNN, then republished on a New Hampshire TV station's website, which is where the crawler actually caught it.

95
00:10:41,520 --> 00:10:46,721
Maya: She's driving from Atlanta back to Honaker, Virginia. Deep Appalachia.

96
00:10:46,721 --> 00:10:51,921
Lina: And she hasn't really been back in almost twenty years. Left at eighteen.

97
00:10:51,921 --> 00:11:00,721
Maya: And she draws this parallel that just—she says it plainly: that was the same age her father was when he was drafted and sent to Vietnam.

98
00:11:00,721 --> 00:11:13,121
Maya: I'm going home to tell my truth. I was 18 when I fled there -- the same age my father was when he was drafted and sent to Vietnam. We both ended up scarred by his war.

99
00:11:13,121 --> 00:11:19,360
Theo: Both scarred by his war. That line just sits there.

100
00:11:19,360 --> 00:11:25,521
Lina: Her father came home with PTSD, and the family kept it secret for years.

101
00:11:25,521 --> 00:11:31,361
Maya: She describes the secret growing inside her like a cancer—those are her words.

102
00:11:31,361 --> 00:11:38,321
Maya: And that's what shaped how she saw home. She writes that she'd grown to hate the mountains—the whole town.

103
00:11:38,321 --> 00:11:43,920
Theo: Because she associated all of it with the secrets she was never able to tell.

104
00:11:43,920 --> 00:11:51,281
Theo: Now here's where the archive does something frustrating and weirdly poetic. The captured text cuts off mid-sentence.

105
00:11:51,281 --> 00:12:03,601
Lina: She's arrived at her parents' house, there's a clash with her father over a song he wrote, and—it just stops. Mid-word. We literally don't get the resolution.

106
00:12:03,601 --> 00:12:10,480
Maya: So the crawl preserved the wound but not the healing. If there even was one in that moment.

107
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:21,441
Theo: And while Christal's return is about confronting trauma—we found another piece from barely thirty miles away, same region of Virginia, that's about a much quieter kind of loss.

108
00:12:25,600 --> 00:12:37,439
Lina: A woman named Melva posted a poem on a personal website called Heavens Gates, describing her grandparents' homeplace in Smith Hollow near Abingdon.

109
00:12:37,439 --> 00:12:45,120
Maya: The house is physically falling apart. Broken windows, rotting walls, vines climbing through the cracks.

110
00:12:45,120 --> 00:12:54,160
Theo: But Melva layers in all these memories—a little girl hunting for hen's nests, riding a bike down the hill, her grandmother calling her in for supper.

111
00:12:54,160 --> 00:12:58,722
Lina: With love and laughter inside those rotting walls

112
00:12:58,722 --> 00:13:02,403
Theo: And the page itself is kind of a rotting house.

113
00:13:02,403 --> 00:13:04,563
Maya: A MIDI file?

114
00:13:04,563 --> 00:13:09,843
Theo: Set to auto-play—a song called "Old Home Place."

115
00:13:09,843 --> 00:13:15,522
Lina: There's also a boilerplate lyrics disclaimer that doesn't apply to her original poem.

116
00:13:15,522 --> 00:13:23,844
Maya: The poem is dated 2009, but the memorial-site design feels older. The crawler found it in 2013.

117
00:13:23,844 --> 00:13:27,043
Theo: The old web had confidence.

118
00:13:27,043 --> 00:13:34,083
Lina: And this pattern—going back and being stunned by the change—it's not new at all.

119
00:13:34,083 --> 00:13:44,483
Maya: We found a speech from a scanned book on Archive.org, someone addressing the town of Holland, Massachusetts. His family farmed there four generations.

120
00:13:44,483 --> 00:13:54,163
Theo: He left as a teenager, fought in the Civil War with the First Michigan Sharpshooters, and was—in his words—dreadfully homesick.

121
00:13:54,163 --> 00:14:00,322
Maya: Then there's another scanned speech—someone returning to America after twenty years abroad.

122
00:14:00,322 --> 00:14:06,722
Theo: He compares himself to Rip Van Winkle, almost appalled at the wonderful changes that meet his eyes.

123
00:14:06,722 --> 00:14:14,323
Lina: He's been invited to dinners everywhere, but he only says yes to his native town of Danvers, Massachusetts.

124
00:14:14,323 --> 00:14:17,603
Lina: Homecoming as time travel.

125
00:14:20,640 --> 00:14:25,681
Maya: So we've talked about leaving and coming back. But here's what nobody warns you about.

126
00:14:25,681 --> 00:14:34,080
Lina: Sometimes the place is still on the map, still has a zip code, and it is just… not yours anymore.

127
00:14:34,080 --> 00:14:47,321
Theo: And we've got two writers in these archives doing almost the exact same thing—both walking through their hometowns, taking inventory. One's in Mt. Union, Iowa, small-town Henry County.

128
00:14:47,321 --> 00:14:56,240
Lina: The other's on a courthouse square in his little Texas town. Both published around May 2013, both crawled that December.

129
00:14:56,240 --> 00:15:00,801
Maya: One from the Ottumwa Courier, one from a Houston Chronicle blog.

130
00:15:00,801 --> 00:15:13,281
Lina: The Iowa writer brings their daughter and baby grandson Max along. Three generations standing in a cemetery, leaving flowers at family graves. And there's this line—

131
00:15:13,281 --> 00:15:19,680
Lina: Tears fell in the cemetery where we showed Max the resting places of his great-grandparents.

132
00:15:19,680 --> 00:15:35,920
Lina: It gave me pause when I finally realized that I love the memory of that part of Henry County. That even though the town is still there, many of the places I knew so well are gone. And most of the people I knew who lived there are gone, too.

133
00:15:35,920 --> 00:15:45,681
Theo: We should say—that column is truncated. It ends mid-sentence: 'I walked past stones,' and then nothing.

134
00:15:45,681 --> 00:15:57,521
Lina: The crawler grabbed a printer-friendly page variant, you can see '/print' in the URL, and whether the full text ever made it into the archive… we just don't know.

135
00:15:57,521 --> 00:16:08,080
Maya: The Texas writer has the same mid-sentence cutoff! He's walking the courthouse square, remembering every storefront, and then—gone.

136
00:16:08,080 --> 00:16:21,601
Theo: And his text opens with the string 'Print67' smashed right into the first paragraph—a share-counter widget the crawler swallowed as regular text. Classic WET extraction artifact.

137
00:16:21,601 --> 00:16:23,921
Maya: Print sixty-seven?

138
00:16:23,921 --> 00:16:26,882
Theo: The machine thought the counter was prose.

139
00:16:29,280 --> 00:16:37,360
Maya: But the memories themselves—so specific. He worked as a soda skeet for seventeen and a half cents an hour.

140
00:16:37,360 --> 00:16:39,119
Lina: A soda skeet?

141
00:16:39,119 --> 00:16:48,960
Lina: A bowl of chili for a dime—and then you just kept adding crackers and ketchup until you felt full. That's not nostalgia as mood.

142
00:16:48,960 --> 00:16:51,120
Maya: That's nostalgia as exact price.

143
00:16:51,120 --> 00:16:55,359
Theo: A dime, plus structural crackers.

144
00:16:55,359 --> 00:17:02,159
Theo: He remembers carrying groceries from the Piggly Wiggly two or three blocks to somebody's house, putting the milk in their ice box.

145
00:17:02,159 --> 00:17:05,999
Lina: A whole economy of proximity that's just… vanished.

146
00:17:05,999 --> 00:17:08,560
Maya: And an actual ice box.

147
00:17:08,560 --> 00:17:15,200
Maya: Now, both of those writers are wistful. But the Andover letter? That's a different temperature entirely.

148
00:17:15,200 --> 00:17:22,640
Theo: This is a letter to the editor of the Andover Townsman, published November 2012, crawled over a year later.

149
00:17:22,640 --> 00:17:29,680
Lina: A man comes back to Andover, Massachusetts after decades away and he is angry.

150
00:17:29,680 --> 00:17:37,440
Maya: The town used to be known as 'Mill, Hill and Till'—and now he calls it a smaller version of New York City.

151
00:17:37,440 --> 00:17:50,400
Lina: He goes through landmarks one by one—the Shawsheen River, historic dams, the old high school—and each one is either demolished, degraded, or renamed.

152
00:17:50,400 --> 00:17:54,480
Maya: He sees the river and compares it to a Love Canal wasteland.

153
00:17:54,480 --> 00:18:03,041
Maya: There's something uncomfortable in it too, though. Part of his argument is that newcomers don't understand the town's history, that they've diluted its values.

154
00:18:03,041 --> 00:18:11,760
Theo: And that raises a real question—who gets to define a place's identity? The person who left, or the people who live there now?

155
00:18:11,760 --> 00:18:14,481
Theo: The person who stayed might answer very differently.

156
00:18:14,481 --> 00:18:25,840
Lina: That's the tension. The Iowa and Texas writers grieve what's gone but hold it lightly—they know the loss is partly theirs, inside them.

157
00:18:25,840 --> 00:18:34,241
Maya: The Andover letter says no, the town itself is wrong now. And I think that's a really human fork in the road when your hometown changes.

158
00:18:39,680 --> 00:18:50,480
Theo: Meanwhile there's a very different kind of homecoming buried in a personal blog post from Christmas Eve, 2009—captured nearly four years later.

159
00:18:50,480 --> 00:19:02,720
Lina: Someone's home visiting family, loves them genuinely, but feels this ache because London has become their real center of gravity. They can't quite name the feeling.

160
00:19:02,720 --> 00:19:15,040
Lina: But the fragment is enough. It's someone realizing that home has split in two—the place where your family is, and the place where you became yourself.

161
00:19:15,040 --> 00:19:17,360
Maya: And those might never be the same place again.

162
00:19:17,360 --> 00:19:27,200
Maya: And then there's a songwriter named Xanthe Littlemore who puts that exact feeling to music—or at least a lyrics site tried to.

163
00:19:27,200 --> 00:19:36,480
Theo: The crawler grabbed preview snippets from a page whose URL slug says 'hurry up with the robots,' which doesn't match any song title on the page.

164
00:19:36,480 --> 00:19:48,160
Theo: Total metadata mismatch. And the lyrics themselves are gated—only preview fragments got through. There's even a Unicode replacement character where the encoding broke.

165
00:19:48,160 --> 00:19:50,961
Lina: We are literally reading through frosted glass.

166
00:19:50,961 --> 00:19:54,001
Maya: Frosted glass is exactly it.

167
00:19:54,001 --> 00:19:59,521
Lina: But look what survived. Under the title 'Don't Say Goodbye'—

168
00:19:59,521 --> 00:20:14,320
Lina: There is a town where I used to live as a child
They tore down everything that was growing wild
And I don't like going back home, I hate to see what they've done
'Cause when I do, well, it makes me cry

169
00:20:14,320 --> 00:20:19,840
Theo: We can't even hear the melody. The full lyrics were behind a login wall.

170
00:20:19,840 --> 00:20:26,962
Lina: A few lines from a song by an artist most people haven't heard of, on a page that doesn't match its own URL.

171
00:20:26,962 --> 00:20:31,841
Maya: That's the archive—generous and stingy in the same breath.

172
00:20:31,841 --> 00:20:38,161
Lina: What strikes me across all of these is that no one finds what they went back for.

173
00:20:38,161 --> 00:20:48,322
Maya: Not the cemetery visitor, not the courthouse walker, not the angry letter writer, not the blogger at Christmas. The place can't hold what they need it to hold.

174
00:20:51,440 --> 00:20:54,800
Theo: So what do you do with the love that has nowhere to land?

175
00:20:54,800 --> 00:21:05,120
Maya: Sometimes the place can't hold the love, so a smell, a logo, or an old object does. That's where these stories take us next.

176
00:21:05,120 --> 00:21:08,960
Lina: And sometimes the longing isn't even for something you actually had.

177
00:21:08,960 --> 00:21:19,439
Maya: Okay, this one genuinely surprised me. There's a blogger who ran a site called Branded in the 80s — basically a personal archive of childhood consumer culture.

178
00:21:19,439 --> 00:21:24,880
Theo: And he's writing about how old packaging, old toys, can hit you like a physical force.

179
00:21:24,880 --> 00:21:29,119
Theo: He's not being poetic about it, either. He means it literally.

180
00:21:29,119 --> 00:21:36,800
Maya: There have been moments where I stumble across something that I completely forgot about and literally doubled over as if I were punched in the gut.

181
00:21:36,800 --> 00:21:49,040
Lina: His example is painfully specific: an Otter Pop, a hot green electrical transformer box, surf shorts, and a friend talking skateboarding.

182
00:21:49,040 --> 00:21:57,200
Theo: He can still feel that box burning through the fabric. That's not a concept. That's a full-body memory.

183
00:21:57,200 --> 00:22:07,520
Maya: But then he finds a People magazine ad for Lacoste polo shirts — the little alligator patch — and he gets that same gut-punch of nostalgia.

184
00:22:07,520 --> 00:22:10,560
Theo: And then he realizes: he never owned one.

185
00:22:10,560 --> 00:22:13,920
Lina: Wait—nostalgia for a shirt he never owned?

186
00:22:13,920 --> 00:22:18,960
Theo: He calls it false nostalgia — longing for something you never actually had.

187
00:22:18,960 --> 00:22:28,800
Lina: He grew up in Florida surrounded by gator imagery, so the Lacoste logo felt like home even though the brand was never in his closet.

188
00:22:28,800 --> 00:22:35,759
Maya: It played into this sense that I had at the time that Florida was the center of the country, the center of cool

189
00:22:35,759 --> 00:22:43,126
Theo: Now — web-artifact note — we're actually reading this from two different tag-archive pages on his blog.

190
00:22:43,126 --> 00:22:50,807
Lina: One filed under 'Lacoste Gator,' one under 'False Nostalgia.' Same post, two doorways.

191
00:22:50,807 --> 00:22:59,207
Maya: And both versions cut off mid-sentence with a bare less-than character — the crawler hit an image tag it couldn't extract.

192
00:22:59,207 --> 00:23:05,207
Theo: He was probably showing us the little alligator logo, but that image is just… gone.

193
00:23:05,207 --> 00:23:08,647
Maya: The missing image is the thing doing all the work.

194
00:23:08,647 --> 00:23:12,647
Lina: That same ache turns up across centuries.

195
00:23:14,160 --> 00:23:18,640
Maya: Right beside this blog post in the crawl is a letter from the eighteen hundreds.

196
00:23:18,640 --> 00:23:27,040
Theo: Its writer is explaining to a reverend named Cochrane why he cannot attend the centennial of a town called Antrim.

197
00:23:27,040 --> 00:23:36,239
Maya: And for this man, Antrim isn't just a town. It's the only place on earth he could call home. For thirty-eight years.

198
00:23:36,239 --> 00:23:42,799
Theo: For thirty-eight years it has been the only spot on earth that I could call by that peerless name, — home!

199
00:23:42,799 --> 00:23:53,440
Lina: He buried his father, his mother, three sisters, and a brother in that town's cemeteries. His only son was born there. He entered the ministry there.

200
00:23:53,440 --> 00:24:04,161
Maya: The homestead at Clinton holds — as he puts it — all those memories, painful and joyous, that give the word home its indescribable fascination.

201
00:24:04,161 --> 00:24:14,560
Theo: And we only have this letter because it was printed in a commemorative book, scanned and OCR'd into a plain-text stream on the Internet Archive.

202
00:24:14,560 --> 00:24:22,721
Lina: There's a stray page header jammed into the middle of it — 'Response of Col. Silas Dinsmore' — which isn't him at all.

203
00:24:22,721 --> 00:24:25,521
Maya: Just the scanner turning a page.

204
00:24:25,521 --> 00:24:29,761
Lina: A page header just walked into somebody else's letter.

205
00:24:29,761 --> 00:24:35,601
Maya: A Hancock County history gives us another voice from a similar commemorative book.

206
00:24:35,601 --> 00:24:44,241
Theo: Reverend William Nichols stands at an old settlers' meeting and says something that could be an instruction manual for this podcast.

207
00:24:44,241 --> 00:24:52,321
Theo: At old settlers' meetings it is fitting and proper to call up the past and rehearse memories and events past and gone.

208
00:24:52,321 --> 00:24:56,721
Lina: Nichols was born before Indiana was even a state.

209
00:24:56,721 --> 00:25:04,081
Theo: He remembers hiding in a hollow stump at age four because a party of Indigenous people came to his father's house to trade.

210
00:25:04,081 --> 00:25:09,281
Maya: We need to mark the frame: this is a settler's account of that encounter.

211
00:25:12,960 --> 00:25:20,799
Lina: His childhood fear was real to him, but the story does not include the perspective of the people who were already there.

212
00:25:20,799 --> 00:25:24,959
Maya: And that missing perspective matters as much as the remembered fear.

213
00:25:24,959 --> 00:25:32,479
Theo: And his schoolhouse — a log hut with windows made by cutting out a log and pasting oiled paper over the hole.

214
00:25:32,479 --> 00:25:36,080
Lina: That's the landscape these people are building memory from.

215
00:25:36,080 --> 00:25:42,320
Maya: Which brings us to someone who grew up in a landscape most people wouldn't think to feel nostalgic about at all.

216
00:25:42,320 --> 00:25:53,519
Theo: A professor named Michael Rustad, writing about the Red River Valley in northwestern Minnesota — flat black-loam prairie, endless horizon.

217
00:25:53,519 --> 00:25:56,080
Lina: I grew up longing to leave the flat land.

218
00:25:56,080 --> 00:26:10,159
Lina: And what changed him was a sixth-grade teacher — Dale Finney Miller — who taught Scandinavian mythology and local history, the Alexandria runestone, stories about the land under his feet.

219
00:26:10,159 --> 00:26:13,920
Maya: That teacher made the place legible to him. Made it worth loving.

220
00:26:13,920 --> 00:26:27,920
Maya: This essay was written in 2001, posted on a RootsWeb genealogy page — hand-coded HTML, no content-management system — and crawled twelve years later in December 2013.

221
00:26:27,920 --> 00:26:31,359
Theo: We have no idea if anyone touched it in between.

222
00:26:31,359 --> 00:26:42,280
Theo: The extraction cuts off mid-sentence too. He was about to say something about learning from his teachers, and then — nothing. The crawler just stopped.

223
00:26:42,280 --> 00:26:44,319
Lina: Again?

224
00:26:44,319 --> 00:26:53,760
Theo: Every one of these people — the blogger, the minister, Nichols, Rustad — the archive caught them.

225
00:26:53,760 --> 00:27:02,160
Lina: But it can't give us back the alligator logo, or the minister's full name, or the end of Rustad's sentence.

226
00:27:02,160 --> 00:27:06,560
Maya: It preserves the ache without restoring the thing that was ached for.

227
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:15,520
Maya: So we've been sitting with all these places people leave behind, and the things they build to hold onto them.

228
00:27:15,520 --> 00:27:24,240
Theo: I want to end with a guy who did something I find kind of beautiful. He named his whole company after a park.

229
00:27:24,240 --> 00:27:29,279
Theo: Ed Romanoff. He founded a company called PineRock.

230
00:27:29,279 --> 00:27:44,560
Lina: And his about-us page—crawled December 2013—has this origin story: when he was a kid in Connecticut, there was a park called PineRock Park. Baseball, live music, giant swings.

231
00:27:44,560 --> 00:27:47,760
Maya: And he just… kept carrying it.

232
00:27:47,760 --> 00:27:50,719
Lina: He named the future after the feeling.

233
00:27:50,719 --> 00:28:01,839
Maya: I didn't know what I wanted to be when I grew up, but I did know that I wanted to create something that moved me in that same way. And I decided that when I did, I would call it PineRock.

234
00:28:01,839 --> 00:28:08,560
Lina: Okay, yes, it's a corporate page—it's branding. But the impulse underneath it is real.

235
00:28:08,560 --> 00:28:14,320
Maya: You take a childhood feeling and pour it into a name so it outlives the place itself.

236
00:28:14,320 --> 00:28:24,961
Maya: And that's what we keep finding. The archive holds evidence of longing, not the thing longed for. And sometimes the longing shows up in the weirdest containers.

237
00:28:24,961 --> 00:28:28,880
Theo: Theo, tell them about the Halloween candle guy.

238
00:28:28,880 --> 00:28:41,599
Theo: Okay. So there's a blogger on Branded in the 80s—writes about retro toys, ThunderCats, G.I. Joe, the whole universe. And he's self-aware about it. He actually says—

239
00:28:41,599 --> 00:28:49,360
Theo: “This junk doesn’t hold a candle to the stuff from back in my day…” I’m not that guy, or at least I don’t want to be.

240
00:28:49,360 --> 00:28:52,879
Lina: He calls himself an amateur semiotician.

241
00:28:52,879 --> 00:28:55,520
Maya: On a blog about Smurfs.

242
00:28:55,520 --> 00:28:58,400
Theo: Which is a wonderful sentence.

243
00:28:58,400 --> 00:29:07,360
Maya: But then he says his year has been rough. And Halloween is the one season where he can let go and just feel something good.

244
00:29:09,360 --> 00:29:19,680
Theo: He ends up at a Yankee Candle browsing the Boney Bunch collection with his spouse, and the blog entry literally cuts off mid-sentence during that visit.

245
00:29:19,680 --> 00:29:23,439
Theo: And there goes the sentence again.

246
00:29:23,439 --> 00:29:31,199
Maya: Fragments keep being the theme. There's a poem on PoetrySoup by Andrew Nashat called "Do you remember."

247
00:29:31,199 --> 00:29:36,719
Lina: We only have a name on a community poetry site; we don't know more about the writer.

248
00:29:36,719 --> 00:29:41,680
Theo: The whole poem is built on that phrase, repeated like a heartbeat.

249
00:29:41,680 --> 00:29:57,520
Lina: I remember the days when all we wanted to do was be together, I remember the days when all we wanted to do was be there for each other, I remember the days when all we did was reminisce our childhood memories, I remember those days my dear.

250
00:29:57,520 --> 00:30:01,840
Theo: That repetition sounds like someone trying to keep a door open.

251
00:30:01,840 --> 00:30:12,080
Maya: And then it shifts. He asks: why is there a block of ice forming between us? Home isn't a place in this poem. It's a person.

252
00:30:12,080 --> 00:30:15,199
Theo: And that person is slipping away.

253
00:30:15,199 --> 00:30:19,359
Theo: Which brings us—weirdly—all the way back to Beethoven.

254
00:30:19,359 --> 00:30:26,400
Lina: The crawl picked up an OCR scan of a printed edition of his letters, served as a text file on archive.org.

255
00:30:26,400 --> 00:30:35,439
Maya: And in this fragment—and I mean fragment, it opens and closes mid-sentence—he's writing about leaving his fatherland and about a friend.

256
00:30:35,439 --> 00:30:41,199
Lina: since I left my fatherland, you are one of those to whom my heart specially clings.

257
00:30:41,199 --> 00:30:47,841
Maya: He's talking about Stephan von Breuning, a childhood friend who has come back into his life.

258
00:30:47,841 --> 00:30:52,239
Lina: Then he says he has to withdraw from everyone because of his hearing loss.

259
00:30:52,239 --> 00:30:57,599
Theo: The people he most wants to reach are the very people he is being pulled away from.

260
00:30:57,599 --> 00:31:05,120
Lina: Think about the layers between us and that voice. A hand writes a letter. An editor prints it.

261
00:31:05,120 --> 00:31:08,320
Maya: A scanner reads it. A crawler captures it.

262
00:31:10,800 --> 00:31:21,040
Theo: Then we find it two hundred years later in a file called beethovensletter001beet underscore djvu dot txt.

263
00:31:21,040 --> 00:31:24,240
Maya: That filename is practically sediment.

264
00:31:24,240 --> 00:31:34,800
Lina: And that's what an archive actually does, isn't it. A company named for a park that may be gone. A Halloween blog that stops mid-sentence.

265
00:31:34,800 --> 00:31:43,680
Maya: A poem to someone drifting away. A letter from a man going deaf, reaching for a friend. The archive keeps the reaching.

266
00:31:43,680 --> 00:31:46,959
Theo: It can't give back the thing that was reached for.

267
00:31:46,959 --> 00:31:58,160
Theo: We can't restore the home, the person, the page—or even the full context that produced any of these. We just know someone was here.

268
00:31:58,160 --> 00:32:01,840
Lina: And they wanted something badly enough to write it down.

269
00:32:01,840 --> 00:32:07,600
Maya: And that the web remembered it—even partially, even broken—that matters.

270
00:32:07,600 --> 00:32:22,642
Lina: Thanks for listening. The homes we leave behind don't disappear. They just change shape—into names, into rituals, into poems, into letters that open and close mid-sentence.

271
00:32:22,642 --> 00:32:24,160
Maya: We'll see you next time.
